---
// FooterLinks.astro — the footer's link row. Production renders it once, inside
// Footer.astro's `.footer-top` (the `role="contentinfo"` shell and ThemeToggle
// stay there — this component is the presentational inner both that shell and
// the /brand specimen render, so the specimen cannot drift from production
// (BL-095 AC-2).
//
// `links` exists so /brand can exhibit this as a live specimen instead of the
// hand-rolled replica it used to carry (BL-095); it is set nowhere else. The
// override keeps specimen links in-page (Breadcrumb `crumbs` precedent). No
// analytics handlers exist on these links in production, so the demo branch
// differs only in destinations.
interface Props {
  links?: { label: string; href: string; target?: string; rel?: string }[];
}

const PRODUCTION_LINKS: NonNullable<Props['links']> = [
  {
    label: 'LinkedIn',
    href: 'https://www.linkedin.com/company/global-strategic-technologies/',
    target: '_blank',
    rel: 'noopener noreferrer',
  },
  { label: 'Privacy', href: '/privacy/' },
  { label: 'Terms', href: '/terms/' },
  {
    label: 'Contact',
    href: "mailto:contact@globalstrategic.tech?subject=GST%20%7C%20Global%20Strategic%20Tech%20%7C%20https%3A%2F%2Fglobalstrategic.tech&body=Hi%20GST%2C%20I%20have%20a%20question%2Fcomment%2Finsight%20I'd%20like%20to%20share",
  },
];

const { links = PRODUCTION_LINKS } = Astro.props;
---

<div class="footer-links">
  {
    links.map((link) => (
      <a href={link.href} target={link.target} rel={link.rel}>
        {link.label}
      </a>
    ))
  }
</div>

<style>
  /* A deliberate optical inset, not alignment with anything. The row sits alone
     on the left of `.footer-top` (its only sibling is the ThemeToggle, hard
     right), so nothing above or beside it shares this left edge to misalign
     against. It reads as cramped without it now that `.container`'s gutter
     ladder is live and pays 1rem at phone widths instead of the flat 3rem that
     shipped before (global.css, ADR-0027).

     `--spacing-sm` and not a raw pixel value: the inset was picked by eye at
     7px, then normalised to the 8px token, because the scale runs
     4/8/12/16/24/32/48 and a one-pixel deviation is not worth standing outside
     the design system for. Anything that changes this token changes the width
     budget below — re-derive it, do not assume the difference is absorbed. */
  .footer-links {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
    align-items: center;
    padding-left: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .footer-links a {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-family: var(--font-family-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-bold);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    transition:
      color var(--transition-fast),
      border-bottom var(--transition-fast);
    position: relative;
    padding-bottom: var(--spacing-xs);
    border-bottom: 2px solid transparent;
  }

  .footer-links a:hover {
    color: var(--color-primary);
    border-bottom-color: var(--color-primary);
  }

  .footer-links a:focus {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    outline-offset: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .footer-links a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
    outline-offset: var(--spacing-xs);
  }

  .footer-links a:focus:not(:focus-visible) {
    outline: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    .footer-links {
      gap: var(--spacing-sm);
      flex: 1;
      justify-content: flex-start;

      /* THE WIDTH BUDGET. Everything here is derived, not measured — re-derive
         rather than re-measure if a label, the face, or the gutter changes.

           available(w) = w − 32 − 53.6 − 16 − 8
             32   = .container's 2 × 1rem gutters at this tier (global.css)
             53.6 = the ThemeToggle: ThemeToggleButton's font-size: 5rem × the
                    icon's width: 0.67em. The button declares no width, so its
                    box IS the icon — a DeltaIcon, so this is independent of
                    theme, palette and font.
             16   = .footer-top's gap (Footer.astro)
             8    = this component's own padding-left, `--spacing-sm` (base rule
                    above). It is inside `flex: 1`, so it takes from the content
                    box rather than widening the row.

           needed = 27 characters × 12px × (0.6 advance + 0.05 tracking) + 3 × 8px
                  = 210.6 + 24 = 234.6

         So one row from 345px up, clearing 360px — the narrowest current phone —
         by 15.8px. Both numbers moved by exactly the 8px the inset costs (they
         were 337px and 23.8px before it existed).

         WHY THE TRACKING STEPS DOWN HERE. At the base 0.1em the row needs
         250.8px and clears 360px by 7.6px. That is not an overflow, but a 0.1em
         → 0.05em step (the same tracking .text-uppercase and .button-text use)
         buys 16.2px and turns a 7px squeak into a real margin. This row has
         already been caught once by a margin that thin: BL-144 pinned the mono
         face from generic `monospace` (0.55em on Windows) to GST Mono (0.6em),
         which widened these four labels by 16.2px and pushed the row to two
         lines on a 430px phone.

         WHY IT STILL WRAPS. 320px offers 210.41px against 210.63px of glyph, so
         the labels alone no longer fit before a single one of the three 8px gaps
         is paid — but that deficit is 0.22px, and the thinness is the point.
         Before the inset the same width had 7.8px of slack against the glyph and
         failed only on the 24px of gaps; now it fails on the glyph too, by a
         quarter of a pixel. Returning even that much to this box flips 320 to one
         row and falsifies the sentence, so treat it as a measured margin rather
         than as the word "impossible". One row there still needs type below 12px.
         The row wraps to two lines and nothing leaves the screen.
         Without flex-wrap it would instead push the ThemeToggle off-screen, which
         is the defect this pair of rules exists to prevent. min-width: 0 releases
         the flex item's `auto` floor so the row can actually take the wrap.
         Guarded by tests/e2e/narrow-viewport-chrome.test.ts. */
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      min-width: 0;
    }

    .footer-links a {
      font-size: var(--text-xs);
      letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    }
  }

  /* Tablet (480px - 768px) */
  @media (min-width: 480px) and (max-width: 767px) {
    .footer-links {
      gap: var(--spacing-1_25);
    }
  }

  /* Desktop (768px+) */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .footer-links {
      gap: var(--spacing-2xl);
    }
  }
</style>
